{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became Count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna and his uncle King Baldwin III of Jerusalem ruled as regents until he came of age.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was involved in the conflicts between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli. In 1164 he was captured by Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim and held prisoner until 1172. Despite this setback he remained a key figure in the politics of the region.\n\nDuring the succession crisis following the death of King Amalric in 1174 Raymond III supported the candidacy of Raymond of Saint-Gilles the future Count of Toulouse. However Baldwin IV the leper king was chosen instead. Raymond III served as regent for Baldwin IV from 1174 to 1177 and again from 1185 to 1186. He was a strong advocate for peace with Saladin and sought to avoid unnecessary conflict.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III was known for his diplomatic skills and his efforts to maintain the fragile balance of power between the Crusader states and the Muslim powers. He negotiated truces with Saladin and worked to prevent internal conflicts among the Crusaders. His diplomacy was crucial in maintaining the stability of the region during a turbulent period.\n\nIn 1187 Raymond III was blamed for the defeat at the Battle of Hattin where the Crusader forces were decimated by Saladin. This defeat led to the loss of Jerusalem and much of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond III died shortly after the battle in 1187 leaving no male heirs. His death marked the end of the direct line of the Counts of Tripoli from the House of Poitiers.\